/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 640 0 R
/E (Identifying new talent is essential for the strategic planning of a sports club's future. For example, elite European clubs actively recruit highly skilled players who can enhance both the team's immediate and long-term success. In contrast, lower-division clubs may sign players with the goal of developing and selling them for profit, ensuring the club's sustainability. Most clubs rely on a network of scouts, which can include former players, journalists, or dedicated fans, to uncover promising talent. The extent of scouting usually depends on the club's budget and may focus on specific geographic areas and divisions.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
772 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 640 0 R
/E (The scouting landscape is fiercely competitive, with all clubs participating, and the one that identifies talent first often secures the best hiring opportunities. However, traditional scouting faces hurdles such as high costs, limited global reach, and the potential to miss out on outstanding talent. Scout reports can be subjective, often based on just a handful of game observations, and may not provide a comprehensive assessment of a player's abilities and potential for growth.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
773 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
